# dao.stream.serve Q1–Q7 — round 2 cross-read (GLM-5.3)

I read codex's round-1 answer verbatim (`collab/1790337700000-…mob-r1.gpt-6-sol.findings.md`) and opened every line it cites. Codex's citations check out where they point (two are off by a line or two but land on the right passages; noted inline). One of its claims moved me (Q5's OD-1 write-effect defect — I missed it in round 1 and say so below); the rest I hold or adopt on evidence.

---

## Q3 — HOLD (opaque ids now), adopting one of codex's concerns

Codex: self-certifying from the start with proof of control at registration, because "an open relay routing by string equality can bind that name to the wrong registrant" (citing `dao.stream.discovery.md:70` — verified: mechanism 1; and design:224, which lands in §7.4's rendezvous text — the register/relay passage is §7.3 at 226; substance unaffected).

The decisive fact codex's fix cannot answer: **the thing a dialer actually trusts — the logical-stream identity — is unauthenticated at this layer no matter what the peer id proves.** The contract records this itself: "Identity is an opaque value nothing authenticates… Self-certifying identities and signed or hash-chained elements live in the payload, above the stream" (`dao.stream.md:1087-1092`). With proof-of-control registration, an attacker who knows a descriptor's candidates can still serve a **fake stream under the real identity**; catching that requires kickoff-hash streams or the Noise-shaped channel — both correctly deferred (design §12, findings.md:284). So registration-proof closes one narrow attack (peer-id squatting on a shared relay) while leaving the adjacent, larger one (fake-stream impersonation) fully open: it is neither needed in the design's stated threat model (dev-only, trusted networks, findings.md:284) nor sufficient outside it. Also decisive: v1 peer ids are **composition-assigned** (findings.md:284); with unguessable assigned ids, squatting requires descriptor knowledge, which is the capability being protected. Relay routing stays string equality under either scheme (findings.md:226).

What I adopt from codex: the concern is real and must be specified — duplicate/contested `:serve/register` must be defined relay behavior (refuse/evict as composition policy), which I flagged in round 1 and fold into Q4's policy seam. Named tripwire: **if a relay is exposed to non-trusted peers during v1's lifetime, the required work is channel authentication (Noise-shaped channel) and self-certifying stream identity — not registration-proof alone**; at that point my position flips. HOLD.

## Q4 — CHANGE (adopt codex's formulation)

Codex: composition-supplied relay admission policy, open admission only as the default of explicitly local/trusted deployments; postage a possible policy, not a settled protocol answer (citing findings.md:224/§7.3, `dao.stream.discovery.md:159` — verified: postage "Accepted" in principle — and `dao.stream.md:285` — verified: descriptors carry no authorization).

I verified the load-bearing distinction and it is sound: the contract's no-gating rule governs **descriptors and `attach!`** (`dao.stream.md:285-292`); `:serve/register` and `:serve/dial` are relay-control frames the **serve layer** owns (findings.md:43), so an admission policy there contradicts nothing. Codex's shape is also more invariant-aligned than Fable's flat "v1 accepts any": policy-as-composition-data with an open default keeps the reference relay's dev behavior identical while refusing to bake "accept anyone" into the spec as a protocol property — and it subsumes my round-1 bounded-state flag (link/peer-table lifecycle, caps — otherwise criterion 5, findings.md:292, passes while the relay leaks links). Postage stays a future policy, consistent with `dao.stream.discovery.md:231-233` (design unspecified). CHANGE: my round-1 position plus codex's seam, merged.

## Q5 — CHANGE (synthesis; codex found a real defect in OD-1's drafting, and its read-truth concern resolves declaratively)

Codex: accept OD-3(a)+(2) and OD-2, revise OD-1 separately, resolve proxy read truth first. Three sub-points, checked individually:

1. "`:retry?` needs no OD-1" — TRUE and I said the same in round 1 (open maps, `dao.stream.md:109-112`). Not load-bearing either way.
2. **OD-1's "nothing appended" default is unsafe under OD-2 — TRUE, and I missed it.** The proposed rule reads "treats it as: not `ok`; nothing observed and **nothing appended**; and not retryable" (`dao.stream.md:836-838`), while OD-2's accepted semantics make an append's effect *unknown* unless clean failure is declared (`dao.stream.md:879-882`). A future write outcome meaning "effect unknown" would be consumed as "nothing appended, do not retry" — wrong on both halves. The fix is one clause: the unrecognized-outcome default for **writes** becomes "whether anything was appended is unknown" (reads keep "nothing observed"). I adopt codex's correction.
3. **Deferring OD-1 entirely — I disagree, on code evidence.** Consumers throw today, not just in prose: `fail` is `(throw (ex-info …))` (`src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:41-43`), and `handle-put`/`handle-next` route every unrecognized outcome into it (`engine.cljc:192-195`, `245-254`; the doc's record at `dao.stream.md:818-821`). Serve relays outcome maps verbatim (findings.md:103), so a source on a newer contract makes this a **remotely triggered crash** the moment serve exists. The right sequence is accept-with-the-corrected-clause, not revise-indefinitely — and note the recursion: codex's own stricter alternative for the proxy (a truthful "fetching" answer instead of blocked-on-miss) would require a **new outcome**, which old consumers survive only under OD-1's rule. OD-1 acceptance is the prerequisite for the stricter design, not an alternative to it.

**Synthesis position (CHANGE):** accept OD-3(a)+(2) and OD-2 as drafted; accept OD-1 with its write-effect clause corrected per codex; add one declarative paragraph to serve §4 naming the proxy's cached-read nature (see claim (a) below). Residual disagreement with codex: OD-1 acceptance timing — revise-then-accept vs accept-with-revision (same end state under the OD process, `dao.stream.md:796-801`).

### The two directed verifications

**(a) Codex's proxy-read-truth claim — PARTLY TRUE.**
- *True as description:* the proxy answers `blocked` on a window miss regardless of what the source actually holds — the design says so itself ("`blocked` also for *not here yet*", findings.md:142; miss → demand → `blocked`, findings.md:137) — and `cursor` returns the last-observed anchors, not the current tail (findings.md:125-127).
- *Not established: contract violation.* The IO model governs a handle that cannot yet know: "an operation that cannot yet know its answer returns the outcome true at the moment it is called, and the eventual answer arrives… as data" (`dao.stream.md:153-158`; also 81-82). The stale-`:newest` argument holds and is test-covered: stale is *earlier*, so it can only re-see, never skip (findings.md:127, criterion 3 at 290), a stale-`:oldest` degrades to the source's own `gap` with recovery cursor, and the proxy "never produces a `gap` the source did not" (findings.md:154). Both readings of `blocked`'s table row (`dao.stream.md:545`) are defensible — the text underdetermines it. That is a drafting gap, not a design flaw: the fix is the declaration paragraph (a transport declaring its outcome production reasons is already the contract's mechanism, `dao.stream.md:446-453`), not a redesign, and it does not touch the OD dispositions.
- Verdict: behavior TRUE; "OD-1–3 do not make those answers satisfy the contract" PARTLY — the ODs were never claimed to; the missing piece is declarative and small.

**(b) My claims — TRUE, now verified in code as well as docs.** Verbatim relay: findings.md:103 ("Outcome maps cross **verbatim**, including the cursors inside them"). Throwing consumers: `engine.cljc:41-43` (`fail` throws), `:192-195` (`handle-put` default → fail), `:245-254` (`handle-next` default → fail); doc record at `dao.stream.md:818-821`. `dao.lease` implemented: `src/cljc/dao/lease.cljc` — fact vocabulary, judge, holder disciplines, `make-judge`/`make-holder` composition constructors (86 defns).

## Q6 — AGREE (positions converge; I adopt codex's two specifications)

Both round-1 answers were lease-governed; the deltas were phasing and shape. Codex's "once grant, renewal, and **pre-grant lifetime** are specified" names exactly what my "composition-retired stopgap" was — the entry's lifetime before any lease exists is composition-governed; that is now stated as a specified phase rather than an apology. I adopt both of codex's additions, verified sound: **(i)** pre-grant lifetime = composition retirement until the grant exists; **(ii)** "a renewal sent through a proxy counts only when its required outcome is established under Q1" — correct and load-bearing: a renewal is an append returning `ok` (`dao.lease.md:207-209`), and through a proxy `ok` is outbound acceptance, so the renewal counts only on the observed source outcome — Q1's amended rule applied to lease facts; without it a false renewal could be counted from an append the source refused. Also keep: the lease must not govern source retention (`dao.lease.md:94-95` — unserving gates attachment, not retention); the judge sits on the serving host (`dao.lease.md:112-117`); the machinery already exists (`dao.lease.cljc`); and `dao.lease.md:286-289` must move from `:ws/closed` to `:serve/close` with `:serve/reason` (design's own flag, findings.md:328). Remaining open for the spec: holder attribution for a remote resumer in v1 (composition-assigned holder id until channel auth exists — ties to Q3's tripwire).

